data.default-value#Default value:
In case that the parameter is missing, we can suggest default value parameters
def say_hi(name = 'there'): print('Hi {}!'.format(name)) say_hi() say_hi('Toto') Result:
Hi there! Hi Toto! Example of more complexe parameters.
Both of them are named as ‘Doe’ by default :
def say_hi(first, last = 'Doe'): print('Hi {} {}!'.format(first, last)) say_hi (last = 'Doe', first = 'Jane') say_hi (first = 'John') say_hi('Janie', 'Doe') say_hi('Johnny') Result:
Hi Jane Doe! Hi John Doe! Hi Janie Doe! Hi Johnny Doe!

data.parameters#Parameters:
Example of parameters using def function
def say_hi(name): print('Hi {}!'.format(name)) name = input('Fill your name: ') say_hi('Toto') say_hi('Peoples') say_hi(name) Result :
Fill your name: Toto Hi Toto! Hi Peoples! Hi Sam!

data.description-in-python-a-dictionary-is-an:
Python Dictionaries In Python, a dictionary is an ordered (from Python > 3.7) collection of key: value pairs.
From the Python 3 documentation The main operations on a dictionary are storing a value with some key and extracting the value given the key. It is also possible to delete a key:value pair with del. Example Dictionary:
my_cat = { 'size': 'fat', 'color': 'gray', 'disposition': 'loud' }

data.checking-if-a-filedirectory-exists#Check:
Using os.path on *nix:
>>> import os >>> os.path.exists('.') >>> os.path.exists('setup.py') >>> os.path.exists('/etc') >>> os.path.exists('nonexistentfile') Using pathlib on *nix:
from pathlib import Path >>> Path('.').exists() >>> Path('setup.py').exists() >>> Path('/etc').exists() >>> Path('nonexistentfile').exists()

